<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tarabai</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1675">1675</a>–<a href="/years/1761">1761</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Tarabai Bhosale</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tarabai was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> queen, the widow of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajaram-i">Chhatrapati Rajaram</a>. After his death in 1700 she ruled as regent for her young son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shivaji-ii">Shivaji II</a> and led the Maratha resistance against the Mughal emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a>. When Shahu was released by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a> in 1707, she contested his claim, and her court became the separate line of Kolhapur. In old age she was a powerful figure at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satara">Satara</a>, where she died in 1761.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tarabai.jpg" alt="Tarabai" width="320" height="414" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Baburao Painter · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tarabai.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Tarabai, born in 1675, was the daughter of Hambirrao Mohite, commander-in-chief of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shivaji">Shivaji</a>, founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> kingdom. Hambirrao's sister Soyarabai was Shivaji's queen and the mother of his younger son Rajaram. Tarabai married Rajaram in 1682, at the age of eight, as his second wife. Rajaram ruled the Maratha kingdom from 1689 to 1700. When he died in March 1700, Tarabai proclaimed her infant son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shivaji-ii">Shivaji II</a> as his successor and herself as regent.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>As regent Tarabai took charge of the war against the Mughal emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a>, and was skilled in cavalry movements and directed operations herself. Aurangzeb's long campaign in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a> cost him about a fifth of his army. By 1705 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a> had crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narmada-river">Narmada</a> and reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>, and in 1706 Tarabai was held by Mughal forces for four days before her captors' camp was ambushed. The historian Jadunath Sarkar judged that in 1700 to 1707 the supreme guiding force in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a> was the dowager queen, whose administrative genius and strength of character saved the nation in a crisis.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a> released Shahu, Sambhaji's son, to divide the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a>, and he challenged Tarabai and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shivaji-ii">Shivaji II</a>. Helped by his legal position and the diplomacy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balaji-vishwanath">Peshwa Balaji Vishwanath</a>, he prevailed.</p><p>In 1709 Tarabai set up a rival court at Kolhapur, and Rajaram's other widow, Rajasabai, deposed her son in 1714 and imprisoned her. She reconciled with Shahu in 1730 and lived at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satara">Satara</a> without political power. In the 1740s she presented to the childless Shahu a young man she called her grandson, who succeeded as Rajaram II. In November 1750 she imprisoned him at Satara, claiming he was an impostor, in a quarrel with the Peshwa <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balaji-baji-rao">Balaji Baji Rao</a>. On 14 September 1752 she swore at Jejuri to keep peace with the Peshwa and that Rajaram II was not her grandson.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>She died on 9 December 1761. In 1927 the ruler of Aundh commissioned the painter M. V. Dhurandhar to paint her leading her troops, and she has been portrayed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marathi">Marathi</a> films and television series.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tarabai” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the Maratha succession dispute that followed the release of Shahu in 1707, <b>Tarabai</b>, widow of Rajaram, set up a rival court for her son Shivaji II, settling at Kolhapur by around 1710.</blockquote><a href="/state/kolhapur-state">Kolhapur State</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Talbid, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. Died in Satara, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=17.35,74.13&amp;year=1730" aria-label="Talbid on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Tarabai"><image href="/maps/globes/g060n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6142 6002h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6142 6002h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=17.35,74.13&amp;year=1730">What was here in 1730? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
